        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How long does a walking pad last on average?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Under heavy daily desk use, roughly two to four years is a realistic estimate. With lighter use, such as a walk most evenings, and regular lubrication, many pads last longer. A neglected pad worked for hours every day may not reach two years. Hours of use and care matter more than brand or price.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What usually breaks first on a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The belt and deck surface wear first, as they are designed to. The failure that usually ends a pad is the motor or its controller, damaged by heat that builds when the deck runs dry or the pad is worked near its limits for long periods. Remotes and app connections can also retire an otherwise healthy pad.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a walking pad all day?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check the manual first. Some pads specify a maximum continuous running time followed by a rest period, because the small motor and controller need to cool. Using a pad for many hours a day is possible, but it shortens its life, and splitting the day into blocks with breaks is better for both the pad and you.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long is a walking pad warranty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Commonly one to two years. Some brands extend the term if you register the pad, sometimes through their app, and several make regular lubrication a condition of cover. Home-use warranties are generally void if the pad is used commercially, which can include an office. Register on arrival and keep the receipt and warranty wording.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it worth repairing a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Cheap fixes such as lubrication, tension and tracking adjustments, tightening fixings and replacing a remote are nearly always worth doing. A new belt sometimes is. A motor or controller board out of warranty usually is not, because on a $250 to $400 pad those parts cost a large share of a new one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do walking pads last longer than treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. A maintained mid-range treadmill is generally expected to last eight to twelve years, while walking pads sit well below that. Pads use smaller motors, thinner decks and shorter belts, and are often run for more hours a week. They trade durability for size, weight and price, which is a reasonable trade if you understand it.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

